/*
  experiments/P8_adversary.spthy -- Profile P8 · ADVERSARY-INDUCED stressor (ROADMAP #6, Tier 2).
  An Attentive inline KDF brings up the session; then a single approval prompt. There is NO environmental
  stressor on the prompt (no habituation here, no base time-pressure on a 'confirm' step). The ONLY route
  to a degraded mask is the ADVERSARY injecting urgency (core/adversary.spthy) on a prompt it controls,
  which fires the adversary-induced TimePressure detector -> Busy -> the rushed user auto-approves the
  adversary's own injected prompt. So the stressor is weaponised, not merely environmental.

  RFC value: this is a real attack VECTOR (MFA-fatigue-style urgency). The attribution lemmas prove the
  auto-approval traces to the adversary's urgency injection. The defence is the same as for habituation --
  number-matching MFA (the shutout mitigation, P6: ShutoutBlocksAutoApprove blocks AutoApprove on a
  Shutout-protected prompt regardless of WHY the user is degraded) -> "implementations MUST use it".
*/

// the attack is reachable: the adversary gets its INJECTED prompt auto-approved.
lemma P8_adv_autoapprove_reachable:
  exists-trace
  "Ex pid #o #j. AutoApprove('Alex', pid)@o & Injected(pid)@j"

// the induced degradation is real: Busy is reachable here ONLY via the adversary.
lemma P8_busy_requires_adv_urgency:
  all-traces
  "All p #s. SetMask(p, 'Busy')@s ==> (Ex u #i. InjectUrgency(u)@i & #i < #s)"

// ATTRIBUTION: every auto-approval traces to a prior adversary urgency injection (no environmental cause).
lemma P8_autoapprove_requires_adv:
  all-traces
  "All p pid #o. AutoApprove(p, pid)@o ==> (Ex u #i. InjectUrgency(u)@i & #i < #o)"
